# WITHIN-RELEASE SCALING: v7.1.3-omp.3 at 1 AND 24 THREADS, from ONE binary in ONE session.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. INSTALL.md carried a table labelled "1 thread / 24 threads" whose
# 1-thread cell came from the HISTORICAL unmodified fork and whose 24-thread cell came from the
# current release. Two builds, presented as one scaling measurement. The round-6 review required
# both ends to come from one release build, and this is that measurement. It also supersedes the
# earlier plan of "add the missing 1-thread rows beside the existing 24-thread file", which would
# have left a cross-build seam.
#
# PROVENANCE (captured by the run, not asserted afterwards)
#   tag        v7.1.3-omp.3
#   commit     e660d19f9fbc212ebfbb2b064b86d9531d61e14e
#   clean tree 0 modified files
#   binary     fdb42109d0820463edaeef132c12e791f4c4a49e5da799ebd79bfe132173ce24
#   compiler   g++ (Ubuntu 13.3.0-6ubuntu2~24.04.1) 13.3.0
#   configure  ./configure --enable-openmp=yes
#   param      9197d394cb3907c83ac3c021af7eef9241952030d2647af2fb045eab8d308853
#              (the fork's OWN shipped param.sdpa, maxIteration -> 4)
#   input dE4  e57a8904d4f35bbdf32b4df9af6175163ca3f46b96a1d8fc58f3adf2956a0be5
#   input dE3  97cfeb508ffeb10d42aebd6cee83f26538b59fdd4325254964cf3405af8a4ad0
#   host       pi -- i9-13900K, 24 physical cores, Ubuntu
#   omp env    OMP_PROC_BIND=true OMP_PLACES=cores OMP_MAX_ACTIVE_LEVELS=1
#   affinity   taskset -c 0-23 at 24 threads, taskset -c 0 at 1 thread
#   rss        /usr/bin/time -v "Maximum resident set size"
#
# ROUTE AND OVERLAP POLICY, logged in the same session:
#   dE4 auto   gate4 fill=13364097 -> SPARSE   blocks_disjoint=0  overlap=barrier
#   dE3 auto   gate3 aggregate=9443183 cutoff=9.20212e+06 -> DENSE
#   dE3 fill   gate4 fill=9551057  -> SPARSE   blocks_disjoint=0  overlap=barrier
#
# ORDER: 3 repeats, INTERLEAVED repeat-major (all six cells, then again, then again) so any host
# drift spreads across cells instead of loading onto whichever ran last.
#
# MEDIANS OF THREE
#
#   cell                     1 thread    24 threads    1->24
#   dE4, sparse (default)    46.612 s       6.399 s    7.28x
#   dE3, dense (default)    176.357 s      22.645 s    7.79x
#   dE3, sparse (fill)       31.370 s       4.475 s    7.01x
#
#   dE3 fill vs dE3 default, both at 24 threads: 22.645 / 4.475 = 5.06x
#   peak RSS at 24 threads: dE4 405.8 MB, dE3 auto 670.7 MB, dE3 fill 294.4 MB
#
# BIT-IDENTITY, CONFIRMED HERE AS A SIDE EFFECT AND WORTH MORE THAN THE TIMINGS:
#   every cell returns ONE objective across all three repeats, and the SAME objective at 1 and 24
#   threads -- 2.1206252458976116e+07 for dE4, 2.0886785242906671e+07 for dE3. dE3's DENSE and
#   SPARSE routes also agree with each other to every printed digit. 18 runs, 2 distinct values,
#   one per problem.
#
# ---- OBSERVED SPREAD, and one cell that does not support three significant figures ----
#
#   cell             min       med       max    spread
#   dE4 auto 1t   46.611    46.612    46.615      0.0%
#   dE4 auto 24t   6.381     6.399     6.506      2.0%
#   dE3 auto 1t  176.289   176.357   176.383      0.1%
#   dE3 auto 24t  22.446    22.645    23.009      2.5%
#   dE3 fill 1t   31.341    31.370    31.377      0.1%
#   dE3 fill 24t   4.339     4.475     4.895    *12.4%*
#
# The 1-thread cells are essentially exact (<=0.1%), so this is not host drift -- it is threading
# jitter on a short (~4.5 s) 24-thread cell. Pooling this run's three dE3-fill-24t values with the
# three from dd_final_headline_postfix.tsv gives six spanning 4.339-4.895 s, a 12.3% spread about
# a 4.533 s median.
#
# CONSEQUENCE, stated rather than smoothed over: the campaign's declared +/-3% noise band does NOT
# hold for this cell. Any ratio derived from it -- the dE3 fill-versus-default figure, quoted at
# various times as 5.00x, 4.92x and 5.06x -- is really "about 5x", and the published wording says
# so. The dE4 and dE3-dense cells are within the band and do support their quoted figures.
#
# RELATION TO dd_final_headline_postfix.tsv: that file is the before/after against the historical
# baseline; this one is within-release scaling. They share the dE4 24-thread cell and agree to
# 0.8% (medians 6.452 vs 6.399, six pooled values spanning 1.9%). Neither supersedes the other;
# they answer different questions and each published table names the one it came from.
